London Crime Data Analysis – BigQuery SQL Queries

This repository contains a collection of SQL queries analyzing the London Crime public dataset using Google BigQuery. The queries were written as part of a practice project to sharpen SQL and analytical skills and explore crime patterns in London.

Dataset
Source: bigquery-public-data.london_crime.crime_by_lsoa
Description: Contains crime records in London, broken down by boroughs, crime types, year, and month.

Project Goals
Move beyond simple SQL queries to answer complex analytical questions.
Identify trends in crime across boroughs, years, months, and crime categories.
Demonstrate the use of Common Table Expressions (CTEs), window functions (RANK(), LAG()), and aggregations in BigQuery.

Key Questions Addressed
Total crimes recorded in London each year
Year with the highest total crimes
Top 10 boroughs by total crime count
Month with the highest crime rate overall
Number of unique boroughs
Most common major crime categories
Borough with the lowest number of recorded crimes
Percentage of crimes from “Theft and Handling”
Trends of “Violence Against the Person” over time
Total crimes in 2016
Top 5 boroughs for each major crime category
Yearly crime distribution for “Burglary”
Boroughs with the highest increase in crime (2010–2016)
Crime types increasing or decreasing over time
Top 3 minor crime categories in 2016
Top 5 safest boroughs (lowest crime per year)
Percentage of Violence vs. Theft crimes in 2015
Seasonal crime variation (summer vs. winter)
Borough with most Theft-related crimes in 2016
Borough with largest year-on-year decrease in total crime

Technical Highlights:
CTEs: Organize complex queries for readability and modularity.
Window Functions: RANK(), LAG() used for ranking and year-over-year analysis.
Aggregations: SUM(), COUNT(), ROUND(), and percentage calculations to summarize data.
Time Intelligence: Analyzing trends over years, months, and seasons.
